22 :db/valueType :db.type/long 536870914] [123 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one 536870914] [123 :db/ident :seon.config.render.agent/max-name 536870914] [123 :db/valueType :db.type/long 536870914] [124 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one 536870914] [124 :db/ident :seon.config.render.agent/max-number-digits 536870914] [124 :db/valueType :db.type/long 536870914] [125 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one 536870914] [125 :db/ident :seon.config.render.agent/sort-bound 536870914] [125 :db/valueType :db.type/long 536870914] [126 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one 536870914] [126 :db/ident :seon.config.render.agent/token-budget 536870914] [126 :db/valueType :db.type/long 536870914] [127 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one 536870914] [127 :db/ident :seon.config.render.agent/whole-width 536870914] [127 :db/valueType :db.type/long 536870914]]} {:id "doc-render", :kind :prose, :value "# Render: what a person or a model reads\n\nEvery view, the model's context included, is a render: the one value printer (`seon.print`), or a render function declared on the value's schema (`:seon.render/html` for the page, `:seon.render/ai` for the context). Nothing else shapes output (owner, 2026-10-21: no clipping functions).\n\n## No clipping\n\nA cut never drops silently. It says what it omitted and how to read the rest. Storage keeps the exact value (an error's message, a running form, a provider's HTTP body travel whole); renders budget it. There is no `clip`, no `subs` for display, no `take n` without its count, and no stylesheet clip (`text-overflow: ellipsis`, `line-clamp`, `overflow: hidden` on text): the render's budget is the clamp.\n\n\n## Prose\n\n`seon.print/prose` shows text as itself (unquoted) under a token budget, sized as the printer sizes (3.2 characters a token, `seon.print.select/estimate`, the one sizer: the history budget and the scripted model's usage count with it too). It fits: the text. It does not: the longest prefix the budget holds, ended after a line break or else a space, then the cut, `…+N chars in M more lines`. Given a source form that answers the whole text, the cut names `(subs source offset)`, which reads exactly the rest (with `:one-line?`, the offset is still the source's). On the page, `seon.render.ui/prose` links the cut to the page that shows the whole, or carries the whole in the cut's title; a printed value's cut names its result handle (`result/h3 at its REPL`).\n\n\n## Bounded lists\n\nA list shown in part is one call, `seon.print/bounded` (items, its bound's config key, where, and optionally the total): it answers the items shown and the line `seon.print/more` writes, `+N more: where`, where being the form or page that holds them all, e.g. `+7 more: ended, all in (seon.task/attempts (deref (:conn sys)))`. Every bound is a `:seon.config.render.list/*` key in `resources/seon/config/default.edn` (rows, newest, brief, top), never a literal. Counts are of the whole, not of what a view kept (root's defects count every one; a card's evaluations count by their ids). Root's context groups what repeats: blocked tasks by what blocks them (`seon.render.ui/waiting-on`, the page's own), contract gaps as one list of names.\n\n\nA render's budget, when its env names none, is config too: `:seon.config.render/value-budget` for a value, `:seon.config.render/root-context-budget` for root's context (`seon.render.ai/system`) and `:seon.config.render/history-row-budget` for each evaluation in an agent's history.\n\n## Tiles are data; the context reads the page's derivations\n\nEvery dashboard tile is `seon.render.ui/metric-tile` over a map (`:kicker`, `:sub` or `:pill`, `:big` and `:unit`, `:series` and `:tone`, a `:body`, `:rows` of `[dt dd title class]`, `:explain`), so a new tile is a map. The writer, fork server and main are `seon.render.ui/load-tiles`, which root's context (`seon.render.ai`) prints as one line each; its newest events are the stream's own words (`seon.render.ui/event-detail`), a cost verdict's lines are the agent's and the page's (`seon.cost/verdict-lines`), and how an agent is reads `seon.render.ui/shown-status` and `seon.render.ui/ended-as` (an agent's card and its stream's stop events alike). A lifecycle is declared once, on its enum's schema, and read through one reader, `seon.contract/properties` (an enum's members, in order: `seon.contract/enum-values`): `:seon.agent/status` carries its live, stopped and gone sets (`seon.root/gone-statuses`, read by root, the web's all=>, the server and the check harness; `seon.render.ui/live`; the swarm's `seon.render.ui/status-order`), `:seon.attempt/status` its finished, judged and failed sets (`seon.reconcile/finished`; a death in `seon.problems/report`), and `:seon.task/state` a task's observed states with each pill's title (`seon.render.ui/state-title`); a task's state is derived once, from the reconciler's index (`seon.reconcile/task-state`), and every task row reads it (`seon.render.proc/task-rows`). A new status is one enum entry. Not this: a status set typed out at each site that asks. A proc's defect line is `seon.render.ui/proc-health`, once: the health tile lists every proc by it, the effect owners among them. One-line text in a row or the context is `seon.print/line`. A test run's words are `seon.render.ui/run-words` and its redness `seon.proofs/red?`; a merge's result is `seon.render.ui/merge-words` (the card's pill and the stream's row); an evaluation's arrow and value are one render for a card's row and a history row, its text a reply item's `:printed` or a record's `:shown` (an error's too: `seon.repl/record-tx`). A refusal or failure shown reads `:seon.error/message` of the one error map, never a second `{:error \"text\"}` shape. Root's view numbers (p50s, transactions per commit) are `seon.root/view-numbers`, read by `seon.root/state` and the render proc alike. A root function called from the page is recorded on main by the render proc when root answers it (origin `:ui`), with that answer.\n\n\n## Health is what flows\n\nNothing is pinged and nothing runs on a beat (owner, 2026-10-21). A render pass happens on an event, an api answer or a tab's request. What is wrong has one producer, what is recorded: root's context and the problems banner (`seon.render.ai/problems`) read root's defects and main's recorded problems (`seon.problems/report`), never re-derive them from the live view; root records an agent's death (`seon.root/end-agent`), and a red run, refused merge or refused spawn as it folds it into the agent's view (`seon.root/set-agent`). A proc is healthy unless a problem was recorded of it: the health tile lists each declared proc with the defects root recorded of it. Series are one point per change (transactions per writer batch, ms per fork, datoms per main commit). A value only a ping carried is its owner's to emit when it changes; until then the tile says it is not reported. `bin/check idle` proves an idle root with a tab open steps nothing, and that a spawn and a commit still reach the tab.\n\n\n## A context read is shown once\n\nAn outside agent reads its current-state block through root's server (`{:op :context}`, recorded with origin `:context`). The agent's page shows the text it last read, made again from that turn's basis (`seon.context/recorded`, `seon.context/render`: a turn records the commit it read and the observations no db holds, never the text); every other view of that read (a card, a history row, the stream) refers to it (`read its current-state block · ≈N tokens`), never repeats it. The agent's own turn and the server print the block the same way (`seon.context/messages`, `seon.context/reader-text`: the attempt sections, then the turn sections, each under 16,000 tokens).\n\n## The model's context: a cached prefix, then the turn\n\nA model request sends what does not change first, so the provider serves it from its prefix cache, and sends nothing twice.\n\n```seon.context/messages``` makes every request in one order:\n1. **The help page** ([agent](agent.md)) as the system message: the same bytes for every agent and every turn (`seon.context/help-text`).\n2. **The attempt sections** (`seon.context/attempt-keys`: the task and its plan, the references, the helpers it does not name, the cited sections, the evidence), made from the attempt's first turn's basis on the commit it read, so they stay byte-identical while the attempt works even when the program they cite changes.\n3. **The scrollback**: each earlier turn's reply, its eval calls and each call's answer as a tool message, whole turns within `:seon.config.render/history-budget`, the newest kept.\n4. **The turn sections**: everything else in the block (the turn, the proofs, refused, the test run, the last reply, unmerged entries, and `:will-merge`, every change the branch's merge will carry to main, from `seon.program/divergence` against the agent's `:base`, its branch's merge base with main (by content, as `seon.program/changed-vars`), never main's head, which shows main's later changes as the branch's: owner, D4), the last evaluations while no earlier turn shows them, and how many earlier turns the history budget left out.\n\nSo each request starts with the whole of the one before but its last message (`seon.context-test/the-prefix-stays-byte-identical-across-turns`), and the plan, its references and its sections go out once a request (`seon.context-test/each-section-is-sent-once-per-request`). Before (2026-10-22), the block went out twice a request, in the first turn's message and again in the current one, and the overflow note edited the first message, breaking the prefix every turn after overflow. v1 measured the same split live: stable attempt sections of about 8.5k tokens, turn sections of about 0.75k, 85-91% cache hits. Measured 2026-10-22 through the server on three open tasks (printer estimate, 3.2 characters a token): the help page is 2,132 tokens, the same for every agent; the attempt sections 1,639-1,887 (the references 2.1-2.4 KB, were 8.4-10.0 KB of whole source); the turn sections 45-76 before any proof has run. A first request is 3.9k tokens (was 4.5k, with 24% of it shared by every agent, now 54%); each later turn adds its scrollback and about 60 tokens of turn sections, where the old request added the whole 3.2-3.6k block again, so with 2k tokens of scrollback a turn sends about 2.1k uncached tokens (was 5.4k) and the cached prefix is 65% of the second request and 79% of the fourth (was 45% and 61%). What the provider served from its cache is recorded on each turn (`:seon.turn/cache-hit-tokens`, `:seon.turn/cache-miss-tokens`, by `seon.turn/usage`), absent when it did not say.\n\n\n## One printer\n\nThere is one value printer, `seon.print`, and nothing is cut before it (owner, 2026-10-22: \"Do not create more than one value printer and no clipping happens before then\"). A value a person or a model reads is its schema's declared render (`seon.render`) or the printer's text; a value that is not text reaching the page's serializer (`seon.web.html/html`), a one-line row (`seon.print/line`) or prose (`seon.render.ui/prose`) is printed by the printer, never `str`. The REPL answers every value through `seon.render/printed`: a value whose producer names its schema (`seon.task/task`, `seon.problems/report`: the value's metadata `:seon.render/schema`) reads by its declared AI render, the same text root's context and the page are made from, and ends with the form that shows the whole value as data, `(with-meta result/h3 nil)`; a map that merely holds a key (a partial pull, tx data) prints as the data it is. An evaluation's error reads by the error map's render (its message, fix, variant and where). The page's HTML renders of the same schemas show the same facts.\n\n`seon.render-test/one-printer-and-no-cut-before-it` reads the compiler's facts (`:form/refs` of src imported, `seon.program/live-forms`; each var's form, and each method's): every var outside `seon.print` that references `pr-str`, `prn-str`, `print-str`, `with-out-str`, `pr`, `prn`, `printf` or `clojure.pprint`'s `pprint`, `pp`, `write` or `cl-format` is named below; every var of a display namespace (`my.*`, `seon.render.*`, `seon.web*`, `seon.context`, `seon.mcp`, `seon.server`, `seon.cost`, `seon.repl`, `seon.agent`, `seon.problems`) that references `subs`, `take`, `take-last` or `drop-last` is named below; and every var outside them that calls the printer with a budget (`seon.print/text`, `line`, `prose`: a cut where a value is made) is named below. An unnamed one is red, and so is a name here that no longer does it. (`butlast` is left out: destructuring's expansion references it in every function. Not covered: `str`, `print`, `println` and `format` on data, and `first` or `drop` on rows shown, which the compiler's facts cannot tell from their use on data; review reads for them.)\n\n### Printing beside the printer: the wire\n\nText for the wire, storage, a digest, a file, a URL, a command line or code to evaluate is `seon.edn/encode` (EDN that reads back, whole: no print limits), and only that var prints beside the printer:\n\n- `seon.edn/encode`: EDN for storage and the wire, whole.\n\nAn agent's namespace refers `clojure.pprint`'s `pp` and `pprint` (`seon.agent/make-ns!`), as a plain Clojure REPL does (AGENTS §4, \"what would a plain REPL do\"); what they write reaches the model as the evaluation's `:out`, budgeted as prose. A check's console lines are the printer's whole text (`seon.check/shown`).\n\n### Cut where a value is made\n\n- `seon.check/shown`: names a budget only to print whole (the value's `:seon.print/full` metadata).\n- `seon.contract/problems`, `seon.task/file-declarations`: a refused value (a function's argument, a task file's entry) may be infinite, lazy or a host object, and travels on the wire as text, so its error keeps the printer's cut, which says what it omitted; the live value stays with the caller.\n\n### Cuts in a display namespace that are not cuts of output\n\n- `seon.render.ui/kname`, `seon.render.ui/dom-id`: a keyword's text without its colon.\n- `my.agent/file-source`, `seon.repl/form-src`: a form's own text out of its file or its reply (`subs` at a reader's offsets).\n- `my.root/task-files`: a task file's namespace from its file name.\n- `seon.render.ui/utc-offset-ms`: parsing `:seon.config.web/utc-offset`.\n- `seon.web/handler`, `seon.server/mcp-reply`: parsing a request's path and a tool's name.\n- `seon.context/near`: the whole line a repaired delimiter is on, the printer budgets it.\n- `seon.render.proc/main-info`, `seon.render.proc/system-value`: a sparkline's points, the newest `:seon.config.render.list/series`.\n- `seon.render.proc/pass`, `seon.render.proc/finish-patch`: the stream keeps the newest `:seon.config.render.list/stream` events; its header (`seon.render.ui/events-count`) says how many older it did not keep.\n- `seon.render.proc/eval-records`: an agent's page shows its newest `:seon.config.render.list/history` evaluations; `seon.render.ui/history-list` says how many more its branch records.\n- `seon.render.ai/section-costly`: the newest `:seon.config.render.list/top` evaluations of each agent its costliest functions are summed from.\n", :prose? true} {:id "doc-agents", :kind :prose, :value "# AGENTS.md: how we build Seon\n\nSeon is where many agents do real work for their user, each in its own process on its own branch, and the good changes accrete into one program that gets smaller, faster and more certain as it grows. This one document serves every Claude session and every Seon agent: **one process for both** (owner, 2026-10-21: \"a self reinforcing system where agents learn from each other be it claude agents or seon agents\"). The same tasks, the same server, the same context from the same wiki, and lessons go into the same sections.\n\n## 0. Start here\n\nYou are in `next/`, **Seon on Jolt, built to replace Seon** (`~/src/seon`, the JVM version, now read-only reference): everything v1 does, smaller, faster and more certain; a v1 capability left out is left out on purpose, with its reason in `BAGGAGE.md`.\n\nRead before acting: this file (§§1–3 first), then `../RESUME.md` (what is in flight, and THE PLAN), then `docs/README.md` (the wiki's map) and `docs/parity.md`, then your task (`resources/seon/tasks/<ns>.edn`) and what its plan cites.\n\nThe running system: `bin/root status|start` (the owner's main tree: server port 7888, dashboard http://127.0.0.1:7777/); Claude reaches it through the `seon` MCP server (§6). The standing root's store (`runs/store`) holds kept runs, which are evidence: never experiment on it. Experiments run in a worktree (`bin/worktree NAME`) on that worktree's own root and stores.\n\n## 1. What Seon is for, and what moving forward means\n\n**Seon does any and all work its user wants** (owner, 2026-10-22: \"Do not assume the system is constrained in what it should do for the user\"): editing files, running commands, fetching from the web, working in any repository, and improving its own code. Software usually grows by tangling, so cost and defects outrun capability. Seon grows by accretion instead: many agents each change a branch, the proven changes merge into main, and the merged code runs next. That recursive loop is the heart of it, and `next/` must stay tiny (about 10k lines of our own code) and fast while it grows.\n\n**Moving forward means the loop works for real tasks, end to end:**\n1. a user's request becomes tasks;\n2. an agent works each task from context good enough to finish it in few turns;\n3. its proofs go green, its branch merges to main, and main runs the result.\n\nProgress is measured as **turns to green** and **tasks merged**, with every v1 capability present (`docs/parity.md`). A piece of work counts as progress when it moves one of those numbers or unblocks a step of THE PLAN that does. Infrastructure (storage, checks, builds) matters as far as it unblocks the loop, and no further.\n\n**The current priorities are THE PLAN in `../RESUME.md`,** in order. Before starting anything, know which step it serves.\n\nHow we keep the first version's spirit: run, then right, then fast; the demo is the spec (if you can't show it running, it isn't done); the system shows what it is doing, live; numbers are honest (estimated first, measured, with what wasn't measured stated); a cause in a dependency (Chez, Jolt, Datahike, konserve) is fixed there, on a `jolt/<topic>` branch; every addition names what it replaces.\n\n## 2. Who does what\n\n- **The owner (Sean)** sets direction and makes the rulings. Bring him one decision at a time, with evidence and, for a big one, priced options (the simplest viable first, your recommendation marked).\n- **Root** (the orchestrating session; in Seon, the root agent) owns **direction, THE PLAN, merging into main and the docs**. Its output is the whole system moving forward, not findings it made itself. Root:\n  - keeps several things in flight at once (worktrees implementing, agents researching, a batch merging) and records them in RESUME's \"In flight\";\n  - **delegates investigation, debugging, measurement, research and implementation**: subagents (Explore or general-purpose) for reading many files, auditing or research; `../bin/agy-agent` (Gemini) for root-cause hunts and web search; sessions in their own worktrees for code. Independent questions go to agents launched in parallel, in one message;\n  - **investigates personally only what fits in a few tool calls** (one status, one grep, one file). Past that, or once a guess has failed, root writes a brief, launches an agent and turns to other work;\n  - checks what agents return (a reproduction, a file:line, a number) before acting on it, and puts the result into a task, the plan or the wiki section it concerns;\n  - merges verified work into main (§3) and keeps RESUME and the wiki true.\n- **Worktree sessions** are Claude sessions each in its own git worktree on its own branch, working one task (§3): they write the proofs, the code and the wiki edit, prove their change, and report. One that has found no cause or clearly correct fix after about fifteen minutes stops and reports what it measured. One whose code contradicts its plan stops and sends the plan back with the evidence; nobody redesigns on the fly.\n- **Seon agents** are forked processes working tasks through the same server, tasks and context (§6). What our worktree sessions find missing from that context is a task for Seon.\n\n### Briefing an agent\n\nA brief is complete, or the agent spends its turns rediscovering. Every brief states:\n1. **the question or task**, and where it sits in THE PLAN (why it matters now);\n2. **what is known**, as paths: the task entry, wiki sections, notes, the exact failing command and its output, earlier agents' findings;\n3. **the scope**: read-only or which worktree it may write; what not to touch (the standing store, `~/src/seon`);\n4. **what done looks like**: the shape of the answer and the evidence it must carry (a reproduction form, file:line, measured numbers, proofs red → green);\n5. **where the result goes**: a file in its worktree or `notes/`, plus a short reply.\n\nFor a worktree session, the brief is its task entry (§3) plus its worktree name. When the cause of a problem is unclear, send two agents with independent briefs; neither sees the other.\n\n### Forest before trees\n\nAt each owner message, and before each new piece of work, root states in two lines: **the goal (§1), the step of THE PLAN this serves, and what else is in flight.** Stop and step back when:\n- root has spent about three rounds of its own tool calls on one problem without an answer;\n- the work is a detail (one regression, one byte count, one log) that no current step of THE PLAN names;\n- a fix keeps growing, or needs edge cases in several places (the model is wrong: back to the plan);\n- the owner says \"forest\", \"step back\", \"delegate\", or asks the same thing twice.\n\nThen write down what is known, hand the detail to an agent with a brief, and return to the plan.\n\n## 3. How work moves\n\nThe same method serves our worktree sessions and Seon's agents: small changes, integrated often, testing the merged result and running only what the change reaches.\n\n**Work is tasks.** All work is a task in `resources/seon/tasks/<owning-ns>.edn` (`my.root/load-tasks!`):\n- `:seon.task/plan`: what exists, what v1 did, the cause, what it replaces and deletes;\n- `:seon.task/proofs`: the deftests and cost proofs that say it is done;\n- `:seon.task/needs`: what must merge first.\n\nIts references are derived from the plan's citations by the one parser ([references](docs/references.md)), and the names it changes are its write set. **A worktree session's brief is its task** (owner, 2026-10-21: process, not whim): root writes what it needs into the task and the wiki sections it cites. What anyone learns goes back into the task or that section, never only into a report. Order: delete first, then foundations (a shared interface merges first as its own small task), then fixes.\n\n**Every ready task runs now, in parallel** (owner, 2026-10-21: \"get rid of all of it\": no cap, no locks, no waiting). Worktrees are truly independent (owner, 2026-10-22: \"Each lane should be able to do anything they want to the codebase, fully test it and then we can shut it down and merge it\"): each works in its own worktree (`bin/worktree NAME`: `../next-NAME` on `jolt/NAME`) on its own root and stores, may change any file, tests there what its change reaches, and stops; root merges and resolves conflicts. Write sets inform root's merge order; they never restrict a worktree. One waiting on a dependency reads and plans, and keeps nothing running.\n\n**A worktree session's method:**\n1. **Learn:** find the wiki sections, tasks and code for the problem; read what v1 did (`~/src/seon`, read-only); research the field's best known way (web, `../bin/agy-agent`), and verify it. Cite what you find in the plan; where a section and the code disagree, that is a wiki edit the task owes.\n2. **Plan in a few lines:** the primitive, the write set, what it deletes, the tests, its wiki section. A change to a dependency or a design rule waits for root's go. No new layers, runners or wrappers.\n3. **Prove first:** prove the idea in a REPL at n ≤ 100, write a cost proof for anything whose input grows, and write the proofs and watch them fail for the right reason.\n4. **Act:** write the simplest code that passes, at the cause, in the owner of the bad value.\n5. **Work it as a Seon agent would**, through the server (§6): attach, read the context, evaluate, `seon_done`. What the context lacked is a task. If the owner will see it (a page, a render), have a subagent with the browser compare the page against the facts.\n\n**Integrate small and often.** Branches live hours; merge main into the branch whenever main moves. To be merged, a worktree session merges main in, proves its own tests red → green, and reports: commits, numbers against estimates, what it deleted, and that nothing of its is running. **Root merges** branches into main (never copying files), in batches of ready branches proven together. Before approving it reads each batch's verdict (a subagent may read a long log for it) and its `src` diff stat; a merge that grows `src` names what it deletes. **Every branch is reviewed before it merges** (owner, 2026-10-22: \"make sure agents are doing good work and not building fragile fences and guards again\"): a reviewer reads its diff against main for §9's mistakes (a guard, a fake default, a clock deciding, a second mechanism, a fix at the reader instead of the producer, an old §7a word, a test that cannot fail), each finding with file:line and its right fix; the worktree session fixes them before its branch merges. The mechanics (exit statuses, what a merge runs, batching, conflicts, pin bumps): [merging](docs/merging.md).\n\n### Testing: compute only what is needed\n\n**Every computation must earn its time** (owner, 2026-10-22: \"I want faster development\"; \"We should always be trying to minimize extra computations and do smarter testing\"). A run that cannot tell you anything new is waste, and waste slows every worktree on the machine.\n- **Run only what your change reaches: `bin/prove`.** It runs the tests and the `bin/check` steps whose reach digest has no green result, and reuses every other (§5, results reused by input digest), so an unchanged tree runs nothing; `bin/test` and `bin/check` without arguments are its two halves, `bin/test NS` one namespace, `bin/check MODE` one step. Seon agents run the same selection (`(my.agent/prove)`, the turn's observe, the judge's `:tests`). Then `bin/check smoke`.\n- **Iterate at the REPL first.** Prove an idea at n ≤ 100 in a REPL before any suite runs.\n- **Long runs only when the work is done** (owner, 2026-10-22: \"Don't run long tests unless we are pretty sure it's done\"): the full `bin/test` and `bin/check 100` (about 10 minutes) run once per worktree session, when it believes its task is finished and its review findings are fixed. Never while iterating, never twice for the same tree, never \"just to be safe\".\n- **Root's merge re-runs only what merging changed:** `bin/prove` and smoke on the merged batch; the full run only when resolving a conflict changed boot, the writer, the reconciler or the build. The full suite also runs once a day.\n- **Never repeat a run on an unchanged tree.** A green result at a digest is the answer; reuse it. A flaky result is a defect to fix at its cause (a clock, a race), never a reason to re-run.\n- **The full runs are explicit:** `bin/test --all` and `bin/check 100`.\n\n**Root's gate, before approving any plan, format, key or rule** (owner, 2026-10-21): (1) which existing mechanism does this, ours, v1's or the dependency's? (2) what did v1 do here? (3) what is the field's best known way? (4) does it add a format, key, layer or rule, and why can't the existing one do it? An agent gathers the evidence; root shows it to the owner. No evidence, no approval.\n\n**Learn as we go.** Each merge's lesson goes into the wiki in the same commit (the section where the problem lives, linked from the others like it). A failed attempt's cause stays on its task. A problem outside your task is filed as a task (what you saw, the cause, what correct looks like, its proofs), never fixed in passing.\n\n## 4. The principles: everything is data flowing through the system\n\n**If nothing has changed, we do nothing.**\n- No proc steps, renders, pings, flushes or polls on an idle system.\n- **Reactive:** a proc reports its own problems as facts and its changes as events. Health is the absence of open problem facts.\n- **No clock or timer decides anything.** Work runs because it arrived, is admitted by its measured workload, and parks when there is none. Time is measured and recorded, never acted on: `bin/laws clocks` lists every place time decides and `bin/laws measures` every clock read, each named in [clocks](docs/clocks.md) with its reason; an unnamed one is red.\n- **The exceptions:**\n  - the cron proc, which only puts a message on a queue at a scheduled time;\n  - a message's delayed insert (core.async `timeout`) when a provider says \"retry after a short wait\";\n  - the kernel's TCP keepalive for a silent network peer.\n\nEvery need is met by one of these. If one doesn't fit, stop and say so; never route around them:\n- **A value.** Immutable Datahike values in one store with many branches. Agents own their branch; root owns main. A record is a fact, and history is kept (v1's ruling; `:seon.config.store/keep-history?`, docs/storage.md#history).\n- **A Flow proc and its channels.** Every component is a step-fn proc declared as data (`resources/seon/system.edn`), with bounded channels.\n  - A dependency is a channel a proc parks on, and back-pressure is a full one.\n  - A run ends when the message count reaches zero.\n  - Procs run on fibers; a blocking foreign call gets a declared thread.\n- **A task.** All work is task entities: desired state on attributes, observed state as facts, `:needs` between them. One reconciler closes the gap at the cost of what changed. Merges, checks and builds are tasks; an OS child is an effect, and its exit is a fact.\n- **A process.** Agents are forked OS processes:\n  - fast (copy-on-write sharing of the precompiled program);\n  - free (a real, unrestricted REPL);\n  - isolated (each on its own branch, its crashes and memory its own);\n  - named by role, task and checkout;\n  - ended when their attempt ends.\n- **A render.** Every view, the model's context included, is a render declared on the value's schema, over the one printer.\n- **An error is data**, flowing to whoever can fix it. A defect holds its message (per key) until the code is fixed. A typed error goes to the requester, agent or task that can act on it.\n\n**A mechanism is built once, and reused everywhere.**\n- Before building anything that waits, retries, admits, holds, spawns, watches a process, answers a request or reads config, find its one implementation: the wiki section where that mechanism lives states the pattern and names its one implementation and owner.\n- If it doesn't exist, its owner builds it; nobody builds a second one.\n\n**Get out of an agent's way.**\n- An agent has a real REPL, and Clojure's own semantics are the interface. Record what happened; never gate what an agent may do.\n- When you feel like adding a check, ask three questions:\n  - What would a plain REPL do?\n  - Am I recording or judging?\n  - What observed thing breaks without it?\n\n## 5. Laws\n\n- **Only documented config.** Every limit, bound or default is a key in `resources/seon/config/default.edn`, with its unit, provenance and what happens at the bound, under a `seon.config.*` schema.\n  - It's read through one resolver (task → model → provider → config).\n  - No literals, no `System/getenv` outside the loader.\n  - Ports too: each checkout has its own (`bin/ports`).\n- **One store, many branches,** as in production. Tests, checks and runs take a branch of one seeded store. A separate store is a last resort, and says why where it is made.\n- **Contracts (Malli function schemas) on every function,** instrumented at definition, and every contract must generate (`mg/check` in `bin/check`).\n- **No guards.** Every outside-world failure point is named in the failure points page (`docs/failure-points.md`), with its one edge function and declared error. Nothing else catches: `bin/laws catch` lists every `catch` and is red on one the page does not name.\n- **Effects go through their owner** (`my.fx/call`, `my.fx/send`). The asker records the outcome on its own branch.\n- **Durability is continuous group commit.** A write is answered when committed in memory. One followed by an outside effect waits for the sync that covers it.\n- **Every definition happens once, where it is defined.**\n  - Behaviour varies through the environment (a forked process holding exactly its definitions) or through data.\n  - `intern`, `alter-var-root` and `with-redefs` are code smells (owner, 2026-10-21: \"intern isn't banned it's a code smell\"). A legitimate use builds vars programmatically in a namespace you own, where no definition form exists (a REPL harness, a test environment), and its docstring says why. Filling a gap in another namespace (a library's, Jolt's stdlib) is fixed in that namespace's owner instead. Stub-by-rebinding is replaced by a test environment that holds the stub's definition. `bin/laws resolution` lists every such site in `src`, each named in [resolution](docs/resolution.md) with its reason.\n- **Results are reused by input digest.** A test's result is keyed by the digest of everything it reaches, a check step's by its build's source, a build's by its sources. Unchanged inputs are never run again.\n  - Content-keyed reuse is allowed for units measured as expensive; no other caches, and never one keyed by time.\n- **Estimates are proofs.** A function whose input grows carries a cost proof. An overrun, or growth steeper than the work needs, is red.\n- **Good tests only:** real assertions, sensitive to mutation, never timing-only, never a placebo a stub would pass. A test's sample data must look like production data, or its numbers lie.\n- **Use the library's words and mechanisms:** Clojure, core.async and Flow (`docs/core-async.md`, `docs/flow.md`), Datahike, Malli, git, nREPL.\n- **Procs follow the step-fn contract:**\n  - a Var with four arities, whose `describe` declares ins, outs, an honest `:workload` and a ping-map;\n  - `transform` returns `[state' outputs]`, with data outputs;\n  - a step never blocks on another proc (request/answer with credit, `{:id :reply}`, exactly one answer);\n  - state is plain data under its schema, and a host object lives only in its owning proc;\n  - buffers are bounded by their loss semantics;\n  - no thread, future or atom outside procs, except an edge reader for a blocking read.\n\n## 6. The running system, as a Seon agent sees it\n\nEach checkout runs its own standing root with `bin/root start` (also `status`, `stop`, and `next`: stop root and start the newest generation's binary, the one the `:build` effect made from the last merges commit, on the same store).\n- It runs detached, on the checkout's ports (`bin/ports`; the owner's main tree uses 7888, with the dashboard on 7777).\n- Its store is kept under `runs/`.\n\nReach it through the `seon` MCP server (`bin/mcp`, a stdio relay to the server, which answers MCP itself):\n- `seon_agents`;\n- `seon_eval` (an agent's own REPL, recorded `:outside`, or `root`);\n- `seon_context` (what the model reads now);\n- `seon_attach` and `seon_done` (work a task as a Seon agent).\n\nWithout MCP, one line works:\n```\nprintf '{:op :eval :agent \"root\" :code \"(my.root/problems sys)\"}\\n' | nc -w 10 127.0.0.1 PORT\n```\n\n## 7. The docs are a wiki, and the system's memory\n\n- **Pages:** `docs/<topic>.md`, one concept per page; `docs/README.md` is the map, and it states how the wiki is kept (one home per concept, a leading sentence that stands alone, patterns in their topic sections, everything linked, current truth rather than a dated log). Sections are addressed `topic#slug` and mirrored in the database.\n- **References are what the text cites, read by one parser**, at the depth the delimiter asks: [references](docs/references.md). Prefer citing a var to a `file:line`.\n- **Merging a change updates its section in the same commit.** Before reporting, read your task's context through the server and confirm it pulls what the task needs.\n\n## 7a. Words\n\n**Name things with Clojure's, its libraries' or Jolt's words** (owner, 2026-10-22: \"stop using names that aren't clojure or clojure library or jolt or whatever related\"; §5, \"Use the library's words\"). Write the right word in every new text, and **fix an old word wherever you come across it** in what you are already changing (code, docstring, doc, task, commit message). A file or command name and a stored keyword keep their old spelling until the rename task moves them; your prose still uses the new word.\n\n| Old house word | Say instead | Whose word |\n|---|---|---|\n| verb | function (by its var, `my.task/fail!`); an `:op` for a request type | Clojure; nREPL `op`, MCP `tool` |\n| lane | worktree session (a session in its own git worktree, on its own branch) | git |\n| landing, land | merge (into main) | git |\n| door, entrance | server (root's request server; the `seon_*` MCP tools) | nREPL, MCP |\n| dragon | failure point (and its edge function) | plain English |\n| census | `mg/check` (the generative check of every contract) | Malli |\n| pillar | principle | plain English |\n| fence | guard | plain English |\n| merge train | octopus merge (branches merged into main in one commit) | git |\n| harvest | stage (onto the branch) | git |\n| stand-in | proc | core.async Flow |\n| dial | config key | Clojure map, Datahike config |\n| admit, guarantees | instrument | Malli `malli.instrument` |\n| stuck | blocked | plain English (stored as `:seon.task/stuck` until migrated) |\n| waste | overrun | plain English (stored as `:seon.attempt/waste` until migrated) |\n| refusal | invalid, explained (a contract's); refused (a merge's) | Malli `m/explain` (stored as `:seon.attempt/refusal` until migrated) |\n\nLibrary words stay: proc, step-fn, transact, branch, merge, schema, registry, contract (a Malli function schema), flusher, group commit, fork server, lease, generation.\n\n## 8. The machine\n\n- **The target is a 16 GB MacBook M1** (owner, 2026-10-22). Seon stays within `:seon.config.machine/memory-mb`, and `bin/check scale` fails past it.\n- **Builds:** `nice -n 15 bin/build`, in parallel, no lock. Full checks run niced, each in its own worktree. Every process on a store uses the same LMDB build. Details, and the disk rules (worktrees removed at merge, no new top-level directories, pruning): [machine](docs/machine.md).\n- **Never flood it.** Anything that makes threads or processes starts at n ≤ 100 under `nice -n 15`; kill your own process past 200 threads. One experiment at a time.\n- **Leave nothing running when you finish,** and say so. Kill only PIDs you started; never `pkill` or `killall`. Delete only paths you created. A process that must outlive a tool call is double-forked (`bin/root` does).\n- **Git in a worktree:** `git -C` on your own checkout only; never `git submodule` in a worktree (after merging main, `bin/worktree --sync NAME` from the main tree); never bare `git stash`; never `checkout`, `restore` or `reset` on paths you don't own; commit explicit paths; never push.\n- **Secrets are never printed.** Paid model runs need the owner's go. Install nothing system-wide. Never write in `~/src/seon`.\n\n## 9. Mistakes we have made, and do not repeat\n\n- **Root doing the work itself** (owner, 2026-10-22: \"you spent most of the session just debugging rather than outsourcing with agents\"): debugging, measuring or reading many files personally, step by step, while the plan stood still. Delegate it with a brief (§2).\n- **Trees before the forest:** a detail chased past its worth with no step of THE PLAN asking for it; guessing at a cause instead of launching agents to find it.\n- **Serial work:** one agent at a time, or waiting on one result with independent work ready.\n- **A clock, timer, sleep, poll, deadline, ping or fixed slot** standing in for a dependency or a workload. \"Never came\" is a recorded fact (stuck, stalled, dead).\n- **A swallowed error, an ignored answer, a fake default.** Unknown is not `{}`, `0`, `nil` or `:ok`, and a proc answers every request it took.\n- **Routing around a principle:**\n  - a fallback instead of an error fact;\n  - a new runner or wrapper beside the reconciler;\n  - a store per test;\n  - an env var instead of config;\n  - regex over code instead of the compiler's facts;\n  - script order instead of `:needs`;\n  - a thread instead of a fiber;\n  - stdin as a lifecycle.\n- **Building a mechanism twice,** or two producers of one view.\n- **Reinventing what v1 already solved,** or designing before researching.\n- **A half-baked plan that got built,** or too many worktrees at once on overlapping work.\n- **A clipping function** (`clip`, `subs`, `take n` for output). Output improves only through the one printer, or a render declared on the value's schema. Every cut says what it left and how to read the rest.\n- **A check that cannot fail,** or that only prints. Every check mode ends on `X OK` / `X FAILED` and an exit status. When a rule changes, read every step it touches.\n- **Merging on a check nobody read,** or copying whole files over newer changes.\n- **Re-running everything on every change,** a full run while iterating, or a second run of an unchanged tree (§3, Testing).\n- **Deleting a record** (a store, a branch, history). Runs are kept, and reclaiming space is the owner's act.\n- **Flooding the machine,** killing by pattern, or leaving things running.\n\n## 10. Done\n\nIt runs end to end through the path agents use; the merged result is green; its numbers are in the commit; its wiki section is true; you can watch it work; it moves a step of THE PLAN; and nothing of yours is still running.\n", :prose? true} {:id "ex-info-http", :kind :error, :value #:seon.error{:type :seon.model/http-error, :operation seon.model/call, :message "The provider answered HTTP 400: the request's messages exceed the model's context window.", :fix "Lower :seon.config.render/history-budget or split the task.", :data {:status 400, :body "# core.async.flow on Jolt\n\nWhat Flow is on Jolt, read from the source and checked in the REPL. Every claim names its file and line. `[F..]` is a check in `../flowlab/docs_check.clj` (run `flowlab/run.sh docs_check`); `E..` and `R..` are the experiments in `../notes/option3-FLOWLAB*.md`.\n\nPaths are under `reference-code/jolt/stdlib/clojure/core/async/`: `flow.clj` (the API), `flow/impl.clj`, `flow/spi.clj`, `flow/impl/graph.clj`, and `impl/dispatch.clj`. Read [core-async](core-async.md) first.\n\n## Where Jolt's Flow differs from upstream\n- **`flow.clj` and `spi.clj`** are upstream's `dev-flow-alpha` at `42dbd51`, plus `update!` (`flow.clj:141-150`). The file header still says \"verbatim\"; that is stale.\n- **`impl.clj` is Jolt's.** It was rewritten in `00d090a5`, `414ce2b6`, `7706dc1b` and `33cb3b3a` (`impl.clj:58-79`):\n  - each proc has its own control channel (there is no control mult);\n  - outputs are routed when they are sent, by a resolver per pid reading a live table;\n  - `update!` diffs procs by fingerprint;\n  - `stop` awaits every exit and finishes in-flight outputs;\n  - Flow's own plumbing runs on fibers;\n  - `:chan-opts {:in {:chan c}}` uses a channel you made (`:105-110`);\n  - an output to a pid that isn't in the flow is reported, not thrown (`:155-161`).\n- **The `update!` docstring is wrong.** It promises `{:added :removed :rewired :running}`; the impl answers `{:stopped :started :kept :lost :missed-exit :ms}` [F16]. `:lost` counts only in-chans that were dropped, never a message a stopped proc held in its state (E3.4).\n\n## Workloads (`impl/dispatch.clj:41-75`, `impl.clj:420-438`)\n\n| `:workload` | the proc loop runs on | transform runs on | note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `:io` | a **fiber** | that fiber | 50 procs add no thread [F15] |\n| `:mixed` (**the default** when describe names none, `impl.clj:421`) | **an OS thread per proc, for its life** | that thread | 5 procs = +5 threads [F14] |\n| `:compute` | a fiber | a cached thread pool, one future per message, awaited with `.get` for **`compute-timeout-ms` (5000, `:419`, `:434-436`)** | past the bound: a `TimeoutException` on the error chan and the step's result is lost, **but the step keeps running** [F11] |\n\n## The proc contract (`flow.clj:189-313`, `impl.clj:417-499`)\n- **describe `()`** is called **once**, when `flow/process` builds the launcher (`impl.clj:420`). Its `:ins`, `:outs`, `:params`, `:workload`, `:signal-select` and `:ping-map-fn` are fixed for the proc's life; a redefined Var changes none of them (V1).\n- **init `(args)`** runs **inside `flow/start`** (or `update!`), with `::flow/pid` added to args. A throw surfaces from `flow/start` [F17]. The state it returns may carry:\n  - `::flow/in-ports {cid chan}` and `::flow/out-ports {cid chan}`, which are **read once, from init's state** (`:440-441`). A port can't be added later.\n  - `::flow/input-filter`, a predicate of cid. Any state may carry it; it is re-read each step (`:465`).\n- **transition `(state t)`**, with t one of `::flow/resume`, `::flow/pause`, `::flow/stop`. It runs only when the status changes (`handle-transition`).\n- **transform `(state in msg)`** answers `[state' {out [msgs]}]`. `in` is an in-port id, or a signal id for a cast. Reloading the Var takes effect at the next step.\n\n**The loop, step by step:**\n- **Paused**, it reads only its control chan (`:459-462`). Messages queue and casts wait [F7, F8].\n- **Running**, it does `alts!! :priority true` over `[control casts & ins]`, filtered by `::flow/input-filter`, with control first (`:465-471`).\n- **A closed in-port** gives transform `msg = nil` once, then leaves the read set [F20] (`:482-484`).\n- **A throw in transform:** the message is **lost**, the state is unchanged, `{::flow/pid ::flow/msg ::flow/ex ...}` goes to the error chan, and the proc carries on [F2] (`:488-491`).\n- **Outputs** are put one at a time with `alts!!` against control, so a pause or stop reaches a proc parked on a full output (`send-outputs`, `:389-415`).\n\n**Where an output goes** (`proc-resolver`, `:148-168`):\n\n| the output key | where it goes |\n|---|---|\n| a connected out | its consumer's in-chan (1:1) or a mult (fan-out) |\n| a declared out with no conn | **dropped, no report** [F4] |\n| `[pid in]` | that in-chan (dynamic routing, e.g. a reply) |\n| `[pid in]` for a pid that isn't in the flow | dropped, `{::flow/undeliverable coord}` on the report chan [F3] |\n| `::flow/report` | the report chan [F5] |\n| `[::flow/cast sig]` | every proc whose `:signal-select` accepts `sig` |\n\n**Fan-out is a mult** (`:126`), so it inherits `put!`: a full consumer costs an OS thread [F18].\n\n## The API\n\n| call | what it really does (Jolt) | timeouts and literals |\n|---|---|---|\n| `create-flow` `{:procs :conns :stop-timeout-ms}` (`flow.clj:89`, `impl.clj:203`) | plans procs; validates conns | `:stop-timeout-ms` default **10000** (`:205`); in-port buffer default **10** (`:110`) [F9] |\n| `start` (`impl.clj:287-301`) | makes report and error chans, runs every init, starts every proc **paused** [F1] | report and error chans are **sliding 100** (`:292-293`): old reports and errors are dropped silently |\n| `resume`/`pause`, `resume-proc`/`pause-proc` (`:316-334`) | put a command on each proc's control chan with `>!!`, **under the graph lock** | control chan is `(chan 10)` (`:173`) |\n| `ping`/`ping-proc` (`:320-340`) | a ping command; waits up to `timeout-ms` for the pongs; a proc inside a step can't answer and is **left out** [F10] | `timeout-ms` default **1000** (`flow.clj:160,176`) |\n| `inject` `[pid in]` or `[::flow/cast sig]` (`:341-348`) | runs on an `:io` fiber, puts each msg with `>!!`, answers a **Future at once**; the caller isn't parked [F6] | an edge must deref it to feel back-pressure |\n| `update!` (`flow.clj:141`, `impl.clj:207-285`, `:351`) | diffs fingerprints (step Var, describe without fns, args, chan-opts): unchanged procs keep their loop, state and queue; changed and removed ones are stopped and their exit awaited; in-chans survive by coord and options | per stop, `:stop-timeout-ms` |\n| `stop` (`:302-315`) | stop to every proc; awaits each exit up to `:stop-timeout-ms`; closes control chans, mult sources, error and report; answers `true` or `{::flow/running #{pids}}` [F13] | a proc parked on a full output **finishes its outputs, waiting up to 1000 ms per message** (`finish-outputs!`, `:356-367`, literal at `:393`, `:404`), then puts `{::flow/dropped-on-stop out}` on the report chan [F12] |\n| casts (`:247-255`, `:290-297`) | a cast is delivered to each selecting proc's castee chan | **sliding 100** (`:253`): a cast a proc doesn't take in time is dropped |\n\n## Hazards found in the source and confirmed\n1. **A proc stuck inside a step wedges the whole graph's control** [F19]. Its control chan holds 10, and commands are put with `>!!` under the graph lock. After 10 unanswered commands (pings, say), the 11th blocks while holding the lock. From then on, `ping` of any other proc, `pause`, `update!` and `stop` all wait until that step returns.\n2. **A step that never returns can't be stopped.** `stop` gives up after `:stop-timeout-ms` and names it [F12, F13], but the fiber or thread lives on.\n3. **Time inside Flow:** `compute-timeout-ms` 5000, `finish-outputs!` 1000 ms per message, `stop-timeout-ms` 10000, and ping's 1000. Each is a clock that decides. Tasks are filed (`flowlab/tasks.edn`: `no-timeout-in-the-proc-loop`).\n4. **Silent drops:** an unconnected out, report and error chans past 100, casts past 100, and a mult with no taps.\n\n## Idioms for Seon\n- **Dependency:** a conn. The consumer parks on its in-port and starts on its first message; there is no start order. A join reads only what it still lacks: `::flow/input-filter` (E2).\n- **Backlog and admission:** the in-port is the queue. Size it by loss semantics (fixed for data that matters). An owner admits by **leaving `:in` out of its read set** while it is at its cap, so the unread queue fills and askers park (R1). `seon.fx/owner-proc` does so (`seon.fx/bound`), and its workers compete for one shared in-port (`seon.fx/worker-proc`): proven by `seon.patterns-test/a-backlog-is-the-unread-queue` and `seon.patterns-test/workers-compete-for-one-shared-in-port`.\n- **Delayed retry:** a timeout channel can't be an in-port itself, because in-ports are fixed at init (`impl.clj:440`). Give the proc one `:retry` in-port, made in init and placed in `::flow/in-ports`. To retry `m` in `ms`, start `(go (<! (timeout ms)) (>! retry m))`: one heap entry on the one timer thread and a parked fiber. **No cron proc.** Cron is only for scheduled work that isn't a reply to anything.\n- **Request and answer:** a request carries `:reply [pid port]`, and the answer is an output to that coord: exactly one answer, `{:value}` or `{:error typed}`. **Credit:** the asker leaves `:ask` out of its read set at N outstanding, and its answer in-port holds N. Without credit, request and reply between bounded in-ports deadlock (R1, R6). **The asker's side has one implementation:** `seon.step/pend` (a new id from the state's `:rid`, what awaits its answer under `:pending`), `seon.step/ask` (the same, plus the `{:op :request :req :route [pid in] :tag rid}` message a root proc sends to the api) and `seon.step/answered` (the answer takes its entry, once: a second answer to the same id finds nothing). Root's api (`seon.root/pend`, `seon.root/deliver-rid`), the reconciler (`seon.reconcile/request`), the render proc (`seon.render.proc/ask`) and the web proc (its waiting pages and its tabs' ids, `seon.web/web-proc`) keep their asks so. A request in flight is the render proc's coalescing window; no clock re-asks: the api answers every request it took, a defect included (task `seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took`). **A throwing step's message is answered:** Flow's step catch keeps the old state, drops the outputs and names the message the step took (`::flow/msg`); root's error reader (in `seon.root/start!`) forwards it as `:msg`, and `seon.root/answer-taken` answers its caller with the defect: the answer its `:dest` names, its promise or `:route`, its `:rid`, or, for an answer a proc took (`{:tag :r}`), that answer again on `[pid :answers]` (never an answer that was already an error, so a step cannot loop on it). The api's own step takes a batch, so it keeps what it took in a volatile of its state (`::taken`, which survives Flow's revert) and answers all of it. Callers park on the reply (`seon.root/state` answers the defect undecorated). Not this: a counter and a pending map per proc, or a deadline that counts an answer as gone.\n- **Failure:** today a throw loses its message (`:488`). Use the hold pattern (catch in the step, keep the message in state, read set `#{}`, retry on a `::reloaded` cast; E3, R2). Send typed errors as answers, not throws.\n- **Quiet:** Flow can't see it, because channels are opaque and ping misses procs inside a step. Count messages: +1 when promised (outputs, injects, answers owed by an edge, a step in progress), −1 when taken. Zero is quiet. A stall is named from each queue's fill, its capacity, its writer, and its reader's read set or hold (R6, `flowlab/kit.clj`).\n- **Never** let a step block on another proc (hazard 1). Make every slow wait a message and a park.\n\n", :headers {"content-type" "application/json", "x-request-id" "req_01J9ZK3"}}}} {:id "eval-square-0", :kind :eval-record, :value {:tag :ret, :form "(in-ns 'app.calc)", :ms 0.22, :alloc-bytes 75552, :shown "#namespace[app.calc]"}} {:id "eval-square-1", :kind :eval-record, :value {:tag :ret, :form "(defn square {:malli/schema [:=> [:cat :int] :int]} [x] (twice x))", :ms 1.362, :alloc-bytes 1224624, :shown "#'app.calc/square"}} {:id "eval-square-2", :kind :eval-record, :value {:tag :ret, :form "(in-ns 'app.calc)", :ms 0.182, :alloc-bytes 75360, :shown "#namespace[app.calc]"}} {:id "eval-square-3", :kind :eval-record, :value {:tag :ret, :form "(defn square {:malli/schema [:=> [:cat :int] :int]} [x] (* x x))", :ms 1.065, :alloc-bytes 1278672, :shown "#'app.calc/square"}} {:id "eval-square-4", :kind :eval-record, :value {:tag :ret, :form "(seon.turn/work!\n '{:task \"calc/square\", :attempt \"calc/square#1\", :max-turns 40})", :ms 892.962, :alloc-bytes 838237824, :fns [{:var seon.turn/observe, :calls 1, :ms 250.028} {:var seon.program/live-forms, :calls 3, :ms 169.691} {:var seon.agent/run-tests-forked, :calls 1, :ms 158.643} {:var seon.program/reach-digests, :calls 2, :ms 153.728} {:var seon.agent/test-plan, :calls 1, :ms 153.323} {:var seon.program/form-row, :calls 1116, :ms 136.421} {:var my.branch/unmerged, :calls 1, :ms 82.264} {:var seon.merge/program, :calls 1, :ms 81.841} {:var seon.program/live?, :calls 1116, :ms 26.82} {:var seon.program/form-of, :calls 1117, :ms 14.221}], :shown "{:outcome :green,\n :turns 2,\n :model-calls 2,\n :results {},\n :uncontracted #{},\n :ms 885.778083,\n :tokens 5151}"}} {:id "eval-square-5", :kind :eval-record, :value {:tag :ret, :form "(seon.turn/work!\n '{:task \"calc/square-merge\",\n   :attempt \"calc/square-merge#1\",\n   :max-turns 40})", :ms 1391.653, :alloc-bytes 1013401984, :fns [{:var seon.turn/work!, :calls 1, :ms 1391.422} {:var my.branch/merge-main!, :calls 2, :ms 1112.443} {:var seon.merge/program, 